    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The beta1-adrenergic receptor agonist xamoterol has been shown to rescue cognitive deficits, downregulate pro-inflammatory cytokine production (TNF-α, CD74, and CD14) and decrease amyloid beta accumulation in Alzheimer’s disease (AD) mouse model. Nevertheless, given these previous results, the cellular mechanism of action and neuronal/glial correlates responsible for xamoterol’s AD-therapeutic effects are not well characterized. Furthermore, few studies have looked at microglia in the context of AD. In this study, a beta1-adrenergic receptor agonist (xamoterol) and antagonist (metoprolol) were separately administered to wildtype mice and amyloid precursor protein-overexpressing Alzheimer’s disease (AD) mouse model, and drug effects on microglial cellular density and Iba1 immunoreactivity (obtained through cell counts and immunohistochemical quantification of Iba1, a marker of activated microglia) were determined for hippocampal and cortical regions of the brain. Xamoterol was shown to have no significant effect on microglial density in either transgenic or wildtype animals. Furthermore, metoprolol administration mildly suppressed microglial cellular populations in dentate gyrus in both transgenic and wildtype animals, relative to saline-dosed controls. Altogether, these data suggest that beta1-adrenergic receptor antagonism leads to downregulated microglial populations for both wildtype and transgenic AD mice, while receptor agonism has no effect on microglial populations. Data from this study also do not support the role of a microglial-density-dependent mechanism of action in xamoterol’s activity as a therapeutic drug in AD mouse model.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
